window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "targets-vs-actuals",
  "title": "Targets vs Actuals (the 8 KPIs)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Update every Monday from week 1 and build your own month-4 version in week 11; it is the Agent Dashboard page Damian and Luke will read first.",
  "body": "## The 8 Key Performance Indicators\n\n- Soft KPIs: Database growth, Prospecting calls, Funnel additions, Appraisals\n- Hard KPIs: Listing appointments, Listings, Sales, Commission\n\n## Jess's template numbers (weekly target / monthly target)\n\n- Database growth: 14 / 56\n- Prospecting calls: 20 / 80\n- Funnel additions: 4 / 16\n- Appraisals: 4 / 16\n- Listing appointments: 4 / 20\n- Listings: 1 / 4\n- Sales: 1 / 4\n- Commission: $3,000 / $12,000\n\nColumns for each KPI: Target week, Target month, Week 1, Week 2, Week 3, Week 4, Total, Remaining, Target.\n\n## Your Launchpad sheet (one row per KPI, filled in each Monday)\n\n- Database growth (geo data): week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Prospecting calls: week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Funnel additions (appraisals that went onto the pipeline board with a timeframe): week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Appraisals: week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Listing appointments (full walk-through or listing presentation): week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Listings: week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Sales: week target [n], month target [n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n- Commission: week target $[n], month target $[n], W1 [ ], W2 [ ], W3 [ ], W4 [ ], total [ ], remaining [ ]\n\n## Setting your numbers (office guidance)\n\n- 300 geo data over 12 weeks is about 25 a week, so a month target of about 100. That is above Jess's 14 / 56 because you are building from zero. It is the program.\n- 30 appraisals over 12 weeks is about 2 to 3 a week. Jess's template says 4 a week for an established agent. Aim there by month 3.\n- Calls: two 45-minute sessions a day at 18 to 24 calls each is roughly 180 to 240 a week. Write the real target from the number of sessions you are actually running.\n- Listings: 3 across the program, so about 1 a month from week 8. Listing appointments: every Advocate at day 15 of their amplifier.\n- Commission: ask Damian or Luke for the office average commission per sale and multiply by expected sales. Do not guess.\n- The app computes your exact pace. This sheet is where you see the pattern: which soft KPI is short when a hard KPI is short.\n\n## Year to date\n\n- Jan to Dec totals for each KPI, plus the average.\n- Monthly, quarterly and annual commission targets against actual.\n- Start this in week 11 so you walk out of the program with month 4 already set.",
  "source": "agent-dashboard--the-agent-dashboard-workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "preferred-partners",
  "title": "Preferred Partners list",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Fill in over weeks 7 and 8, three calls a week, until all ten partners have a name, a number and an agreed client discount.",
  "body": "> Every seller has a list of jobs before the home goes on the market. Hand them the people who do them. That is value no other agent has given them.\n\n> Negotiate a 10% discount for your clients with every partner below. It is one more point of difference.\n\n## The ten partners\n\n- Painter. Fresh walls and touch ups before the photos. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Handyman. The list of small fixes buyers notice at the open. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Landscaper / gardener. Kerb appeal. The front garden is the first photo. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Builder. Bigger repairs, and what is worth doing before you sell. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Cleaner. A deep clean before the photos and before the first open. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Stylist. Furniture and styling for the photos and the opens. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Building and pest. No surprises once a buyer is serious. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Conveyancer / solicitor. The contract ready before the launch date. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Mortgage broker. Buyers who know exactly what they can spend. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n- Removalist / storage. Space to show the home, and the move itself. My person: [name, number]. 10% agreed: [yes / no]\n\n## The three partners I will call this week to negotiate the 10% discount\n\n- 1. [partner, name]\n- 2. [partner, name]\n- 3. [partner, name]\n\n## Office guidance\n\n- Check with Damian or Luke first. The office may already have a broker, conveyancer and storage partner; they are the ones named in the Broker, Legal and Storage letters. Use those.\n- Where the list is used: the Presentation letter (day 14), the Legal letter (day 18), the Broker letter (day 21), the Storage letter (day 24), and every \"what should I fix before I sell?\" conversation at the door.\n- Ask each partner to send work back. A painter who is in three Mildura homes a week meets sellers before you do.",
  "source": "30-day-lead-amplifier--the-30-day-lead-amplifier-workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "video-bank",
  "title": "The Video Bank (six evergreen videos)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Write four bullet points under each video in week 8, film all six in one sitting, and drop the links into the amplifier days that call for a video.",
  "body": "> Six videos you can film once and drop into every plan as a link. Write your four bullet points under each, then film them in one sitting.\n\n## The six\n\n- Education: MARKET UPDATE. \"[number] sales this week, [number] people through the opens. Here is what it means if you are thinking of selling.\" Four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed: [date]\n- Fears: WHAT YOU GET FOR THE FEE. \"What happens between the day you list and the day you settle, and who does it.\" Four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed: [date]\n- Proof: A RECENT RESULT. \"[address]. What the owners wanted, what we did, and how it finished.\" Four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed: [date]\n- Fears: HOW TO CHOOSE AN AGENT. \"Three questions to ask any agent before you sign. Including me.\" Four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed: [date]\n- Personality: WHY I DO THIS. \"Thirty seconds on why I love this work, and what my clients can count on.\" Four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed: [date]\n- Advocates: THE ASK. \"We just sold [address] for [result]. Buyers are still looking in your area. Let's catch up this week.\" Four points: [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ]. Filmed: [date]\n\n## Six steps (Key 5: Package and send)\n\n- 01 Draft the script with Claude or ChatGPT.\n- 02 Ask for it as four bullet points.\n- 03 Open VideoAsk on iPhone, or BigVU on Android.\n- 04 Paste the bullet points into the notes on screen.\n- 05 Press record and talk to the points. One take.\n- 06 Share the link by SMS, email or your CRM.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Under sixty seconds.\n- Talk to one person, not a crowd.\n- Good light, quiet room, phone at eye level.\n- Done beats perfect. Send the first one this week.\n\n## Office guidance for a recruit\n\n- The Market Update is your first video (week 4) and your weekly one from week 8. The numbers come from the office's weekly results; check every one before you film.\n- A Recent Result: use an office sale with the listing agent's OK, and say it is the office's result, not yours.\n- The Market Update ages in a week. The other five are evergreen; film them once, properly, and re-use the links for months.\n- Where the links live: the amplifier days that call for a video (Skeptics days 4 and 17, Audience days 4 and 17, Advocates day 7).",
  "source": "30-day-lead-amplifier--the-30-day-lead-amplifier-workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "letter-bank-schedule",
  "title": "The Letter Bank schedule",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Personalise all six letters in week 6, get each signed off as Ready, then run the drip on every appraisal by group and day.",
  "body": "> Six letters, already written. Each one speaks to one emotion and goes out on a set day. Nobody sends letters any more, which is why they get read.\n\n## The schedule\n\n- Day 2: INTRODUCTION. Emotion: personality. Who gets it: Skeptics, Audience. What it does: thanks them, and tells them you do things differently. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 9: TESTIMONIAL. Emotion: proof. Who gets it: Skeptics. What it does: other sellers in their own words, with numbers to call. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 14: PRESENTATION. Emotion: education. Who gets it: Audience, Advocates. What it does: tips to present the home, plus your painter, handyman, landscaper and builder. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 18: LEGAL. Emotion: fears. Who gets it: Advocates. What it does: who does the legal work, and your local conveyancers. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 21: BROKER. Emotion: fears. Who gets it: Advocates. What it does: why a broker in the campaign helps buyers pay more. Ready: [ ]\n- Day 24: STORAGE. Emotion: education. Who gets it: Advocates. What it does: space to present the home, with a client offer. Ready: [ ]\n\n> Put your own name, number and preferred partners in the brackets, then load them into your CRM with the three amplifiers.\n\n## Launchpad schedule (office guidance)\n\n- Day 0 is the appraisal or the visit. Day 2 means two days after you met them.\n- Week 6: personalise all six with Ray White Mildura details, your name, title and number, the broker partner and the storage partner. Damian or Luke sign each one off as Ready before it goes.\n- Enclosures to build once: the presentation-tips sheet, three conveyancers with contact names, three to five office testimonials with permission and numbers, the broker and storage offers.\n- Jess lists letters and email under 'Integration' beside her phone and visit touch points: the letter sets up the call. About a week after each letter lands, your touch-point call asks whether it arrived and what they thought of it.\n- Hand-write the envelopes. Post, do not drop, unless you are already knocking that street.",
  "source": "30-day-lead-amplifier--the-30-day-lead-amplifier-workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "amplifier-sequences",
  "title": "The three 30-day amplifiers (day by day)",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Load all three into the CRM in week 8 and drop every new lead into the right one the day you meet them; tick each touch as it is done.",
  "body": "> Three amplifiers, one for each group, across six modes. Load them into your CRM once, and every new lead goes straight in the day you meet them.\n\nModes: video, email, SMS, letter, phone, face to face.\n\n## Skeptics (give only)\n\n- Day 1: Call. Thank them for coming through.\n- Day 2: Letter. Introduction.\n- Day 4: Video. Market update.\n- Day 7: SMS. One useful fact about their suburb.\n- Day 9: Letter. Testimonials, with numbers to call.\n- Day 11: Email. How to choose an agent.\n- Day 17: Video. What an agent does for the fee.\n- Day 25: Email. A review in the client's words.\n- Day 28: Call. Share a recent sale near them.\n\n## Audience (give, then ask)\n\n- Day 1: Call. What has them thinking about selling?\n- Day 2: Letter. Introduction.\n- Day 4: Video. Personal market update.\n- Day 7: Call. Answer any questions.\n- Day 11: Email. Your selling process, one page.\n- Day 14: Letter. Presentation, with your partners.\n- Day 17: Video. The fear they raised, answered.\n- Day 21: Call. Offer to walk through the home.\n- Day 28: Call. Ask for the appraisal.\n\n## Advocates (ask)\n\n- Day 1: Call. Book the appraisal.\n- Day 4: SMS. Confirm the time.\n- Day 7: Video. What to expect on the day.\n- Day 11: Email. A recent result and why it worked.\n- Day 14: Letter. Presentation, with your partners.\n- Day 15: Call. Ask for the listing.\n- Day 18: Letter. Legal, with your conveyancers.\n- Day 21: Letter. Broker.\n- Day 24: Letter. Storage.\n- Day 25: Call. Ask who else is thinking of selling.\n- Day 28: Letter. Thank you, handwritten.\n\n> These are starting points. Change the days and the modes to suit your market. Keep the order: give first, ask last.\n\n- Who runs this in my team: [name]\n- Loaded into the CRM by: [name, date]\n\n## How it fits 300 / 30 / 3 (office guidance)\n\n- Day 0 is the day you meet them: at the door, at an open home, on a call. Tag the group on the short form and drop them in the same day.\n- A geo-data contact with no stated need is usually a Skeptic. An appraisal with a 2 to 12 month timeframe is usually Audience. A 0 to 2 month appraisal, or anyone who asked you for the appraisal, is an Advocate.\n- Moving people up is the job. A Skeptic who replies becomes Audience. An Audience member who says yes to the day 28 appraisal becomes an Advocate and starts the Advocates plan the next day.\n- The Advocates plan is your standard post-appraisal checklist for the 30 appraisals. Day 15 is where the 3 listings come from.\n- One line on Jess's two-column page (\"Day 7: Call. Answer any questions.\") could sit under either Audience or Advocates. We have placed it with Audience. Check the printed workbook if in doubt.",
  "source": "30-day-lead-amplifier--the-30-day-lead-amplifier-workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "lost-listing-case-study",
  "title": "Lost Listing Case Study and post-mortem",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Complete in week 6 for every unsuccessful appraisal and leaked farm sale to date, and again at graduation; the contact key is the first thing to check.",
  "body": "## Part A: Lost Listing Case Study (Sales is a Contact Sport)\n\nOne row for each listing lost in the farm: an appraisal that listed with another agent, or a farm sale where the owner was in our database.\n\n- Lost listing identification: [address or name]\n- Month: [month it listed]\n- Timeframe to list (when you met them against when they listed): [0 to 2 months / 2 to 12 months]\n- Reason for loss: [what you think happened]\n\nContact key: 0 to 2 months, weekly. 2 to 12 months, monthly.\n\nAsk of every row: were they touched at the key? If a 0 to 2 month seller went three weeks without a touch, you have found the reason.\n\n## Part B: Your last three lost listings (30-Day Lead Amplifier)\n\n> Write them down. Circle the group they were in when you met them. Then tick only the modes you used consistently.\n\n- 1. Address or name: [ ]. Group: S / AU / AD. Modes used consistently: video / email / SMS / letter / phone / face to face. Why you think you lost it: [ ]\n- 2. Address or name: [ ]. Group: S / AU / AD. Modes used consistently: video / email / SMS / letter / phone / face to face. Why you think you lost it: [ ]\n- 3. Address or name: [ ]. Group: S / AU / AD. Modes used consistently: video / email / SMS / letter / phone / face to face. Why you think you lost it: [ ]\n\nS = Skeptic, AU = Audience, AD = Advocate.\n\nWhat I was not doing:\n\n- 1. [ ]\n- 2. [ ]\n- 3. [ ]\n\n## Office guidance\n\n- A recruit's lost listings include the ones that never reached an appraisal: the geo-data contact who said \"maybe next year\" in week 2 and listed with someone else in week 9. That is a leak, and it counts.\n- The pattern to look for is always the same: one mode only (phone and nothing else), or touches that stopped after the appraisal. Part B makes it visible.\n- The workbook page \"Lost Listings - 4 Triggers\" is filled in live in Jess's session and is blank in the printed copy. Ask Damian or Luke for the four; do not guess them.\n- Bring both parts to the week 6 and week 12 reviews. The \"What I was not doing\" lines become next month's drills.",
  "source": "sales-is-a-contact-sport--workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "leakage-audit",
  "title": "Identify the Leakage: last 3 months",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Do it in week 1 to see where the farm's sales are going, and again in week 12; the leak is every sale where the owner was in our database and listed elsewhere.",
  "body": "## Jess's maths: Datashare = Market Share\n\n- 1000 geographical contacts\n- 5% average will transact per year (national average per suburb per year)\n- 50 sales x $10K\n- = $500K\n- Each contact is worth $500\n\n## Your farm\n\n- Homes in my farm: [number]\n- At 5%, sales a year in the farm: [homes x 0.05]\n- Office average commission per sale: $[ask Damian or Luke]\n- Income available in the farm each year: [sales x commission]\n- Contacts I hold today: [number]\n- At 300 contacts on Jess's $500 figure: $150,000 a year of future income sitting in your CRM (300 x $500, her assumptions, your arithmetic)\n\n## Identify the leakage: last 3 months\n\nOne row for every sale in the farm in the last three months, any agency.\n\n- 1. Address: [ ]. Sold by: [agency]. Sale date: [ ]. Owner in our database: Yes / No. Any touch from us in the last 12 months: Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n- 2. Address: [ ]. Sold by: [agency]. Sale date: [ ]. Owner in our database: Yes / No. Any touch from us in the last 12 months: Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n- 3. Address: [ ]. Sold by: [agency]. Sale date: [ ]. Owner in our database: Yes / No. Any touch from us in the last 12 months: Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n- 4. Address: [ ]. Sold by: [agency]. Sale date: [ ]. Owner in our database: Yes / No. Any touch from us in the last 12 months: Yes / No. What we could have done: [ ]\n\n## Totals\n\n- Sales in the farm, last 3 months: [n]\n- Sold by Ray White Mildura: [n]\n- Owner was in our database but listed elsewhere: [n] (this is the leak)\n- Owner was not in any database: [n] (this is the geo data you do not have yet)\n\n## What to do with it\n\n- Every leaked address gets a Just Sold door knock and the Magic 50 calls around it. The owners who sold are gone; their neighbours are not.\n- Every \"not in any database\" address tells you which streets go first on the Appraisal Drive calendar.\n- Jess's second big key is \"Plug the leaks\". Redo this in week 12. The number in \"owner in our database but listed elsewhere\" should be zero.",
  "source": "client-connections--the-client-connection-model-interactive.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "capacity-tiers",
  "title": "Build To Your Capacity: Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Tier every contact in week 6 and again in week 12, then schedule the annual appraisal for Silver and above; promise only the cadence you can deliver.",
  "body": "Jess's annual cadence per contact, by tier.\n\n## Bronze\n\n- Communication: 2 x calls per year\n- Communication: 2 x 5 Star Prospect messages\n- Information: 2 x newsletters per year\n- Information: weekly email update (active pipe)\n\n## Silver\n\n- Communication: annual appraisal\n- Communication: 4 calls per year\n- Communication: 2 x 5 Star Prospect messages\n- Communication: monthly SMS\n- Communication: 1 x hand written card (thank you / anniversary)\n- Information: 4 x newsletters per year\n- Information: weekly email update (active pipe)\n- Information: 1 x hard copy newsletter\n\n## Gold\n\n- Communication: annual appraisal\n- Communication: 4 calls per year\n- Communication: 4 x 5 Star Prospect messages\n- Communication: monthly SMS\n- Communication: monthly video update\n- Communication: 2 x hand written cards (thank you / anniversary)\n- Communication: Christmas gift\n- Information: 6 x newsletters per year\n- Information: weekly email update (active pipe)\n- Information: 2 x hard copy newsletters\n\n## Platinum\n\n- Communication: annual appraisal\n- Communication: 4 calls per year\n- Communication: 6 x 5 Star Prospect messages\n- Communication: monthly video update\n- Communication: quarterly movie night\n- Communication: 2 x hand written cards (thank you / anniversary)\n- Communication: Christmas gift\n- Information: 12 x newsletters per year\n- Information: weekly email update (active pipe)\n- Information: bi-monthly community newsletter\n\nThe workbook page is a four-column table and the Silver, Gold and Platinum columns above were rebuilt from the text extraction. Check them against the printed workbook before you lock the cadence into the CRM.\n\n## Who goes where (office guidance)\n\n- Bronze: geo-data contact, no stated need, no appraisal yet.\n- Silver: appraised, 2 to 12 month timeframe (Audience).\n- Gold: appraised, 0 to 2 months, or an Advocate; past office clients you have been handed.\n- Platinum: your own past clients and anyone who has referred you a lead. You will earn these.\n\n## Your worksheet\n\n- Contacts in Bronze: [n]. Silver: [n]. Gold: [n]. Platinum: [n].\n- Communication touches this creates in a year, counted from the matrix above: Bronze 4 each, Silver about 20 each, Gold about 36 each, Platinum about 26 each plus the quarterly movie nights.\n- 300 Bronze contacts is 1,200 communication touches a year: 600 5 Star Prospect messages, which the CRM can send, and 600 calls, which are you. The newsletters and weekly email are on top and are automated.\n- Can you deliver it? \"Build to your capacity\" means the tier you can actually service, not the tier you wish for. Promise less and deliver all of it.\n- Communication over information: a call, a card or an appraisal builds a relationship. A newsletter on its own does not. Keep the communication lines, automate the information lines.",
  "source": "client-connections--the-client-connection-model-interactive.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "ai-message-prompt",
  "title": "AI message prompt (Claude or ChatGPT)",
  "kind": "worksheet",
  "when": "Run it once per lead group in week 7 to draft your 12 messages (four emotions by three groups), then read every draft out loud and check every number before it goes.",
  "body": "## Paste this into Claude or ChatGPT\n\n> You are writing listing lead communication for my real estate business.\n> ME AND MY MARKET: [my name, my business, the suburbs I sell in, my recent results].\n> THIS LEAD IS A: [skeptic with no trust / audience member who knows me / advocate who is ready].\n> Write four messages for this lead, one for each emotion: their fears, proof, education, and my personality.\n> For a skeptic, give value only. No ask. For an audience member, give first, then a soft ask. For an advocate, a clear ask.\n> For each message give me a video script as four bullet points I can talk to, and an email under one hundred and twenty words with a subject line.\n> Use my real numbers where I give them. If you do not have a number, leave a [bracket] for me to fill in. Do not make up statistics.\n> Write the way I speak. Short. No marketing language. Nothing that sounds like a sales pitch.\n> Australian English.\n\n## Then ask for these\n\n- Put this into four bullet points for a video.\n- Make the email shorter, and warmer.\n- Rewrite this for an SMS under three hundred characters.\n- Add my numbers: [sales, median price, open home numbers].\n\n## The rules\n\n- AI drafts it. You read it out loud before it goes.\n- Check every number against your own data before you send.\n- Done beats perfect. Get the first one out.\n\nThe first message I will build: [group, emotion, lead's first name]\n\n## Office guidance\n\n- Run it three times, once per group, to get your 12 messages. Save them as CRM templates so the amplifiers can send them.\n- \"My business\" is Ray White Mildura and \"the suburbs I sell in\" are your farm, for example Mildura, Irymple, Red Cliffs or Merbein. \"My recent results\" are the office's until you have your own; say so in the brackets.\n- Your numbers come from the office: weekly sales, open home attendance, days on market. Ask Damian or Luke. Never let the tool invent one, and never send a draft with a [bracket] still in it.\n- Do not paste a client's full name, address, phone or email into the tool. First name only, and add the details back in the CRM.\n- Read it out loud. If it does not sound like you at the door, change it until it does.",
  "source": "30-day-lead-amplifier--the-30-day-lead-amplifier-workbook.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "pre-visit-checklist",
  "title": "Pre-visit checklist (before every door, drop or appraisal)",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Run it the night before and in the car before every market-report drop, curbside or 15-minute appraisal.",
  "body": "Office guidance, built from Jess's Front Door Script, the Train To Prospect script and the appraisal forms.\n\n## The night before\n\n- The street or the address is on this week's Appraisal Drive calendar and the letter landed 3 to 5 days ago.\n- Three or four comparable sales from the last six months, written down, with a conservative range for each home you expect to see.\n- Market report printed, one per door, plus spares. Business cards.\n- Short forms (Market Update Appraisal) in the folder, or the app open on the phone.\n- CRM check: is this owner already in the system? Any notes from a call? A settlement date?\n- Booked appointments confirmed and the 30-minutes-prior SMS scheduled.\n\n## In the car\n\n- Read the Front Door Script once, out loud: the report drop, the two options (curbside or quick walk through), the \"another appointment in 20 mins\" line, the conservative range, seed the full assessment, \"I'll keep in touch\".\n- Decide the first door and the direction you walk. Start at the end of the street with the most comparable sales.\n- Phone on silent, notepad in your pocket, forms in the folder.\n\n## At the door\n\n- Your name, Ray White Mildura and the reason, inside ten seconds.\n- Respect \"do not knock\" and \"no canvassing\" signs. Leave the report in the letterbox and move on.\n- Two options, their choice: curbside now, or a quick walk through. Never push the walk through.\n- A range, never a single figure, and never a promise of price. It is an appraisal, not a valuation.\n- Ask Jess's question: \"Are there any real estate needs that we can assist you with in the next 12 months?\" and collect name, address, phone, email.\n- Anything that needs a follow up gets two times offered on the spot.\n\n## Before you drive off (2 minutes)\n\n- Short form done: action plan, category, precinct, lead group.\n- Geo data added or confirmed in the CRM, with the settlement date if they told you.\n- Appraisal logged in the app. 2-hours-after SMS set. 12-month letter and anniversary SMS dates set. Day 2 Introduction letter queued.\n- Pipeline card created with the next touch date and the amplifier chosen.\n\n## Compliance line (office guidance)\n\n- We give market appraisals, not valuations. Only a licensed valuer gives a valuation.\n- The exclusive-agency note stays on every letter that carries it (Appraisal Drive, 12 Month Re-Appraisal Automation, Just Listed, Just Sold). If another agent has a current exclusive agency on the property, we do not interfere.\n- Anyone who asks not to be contacted is marked \"do not contact\" in the CRM that day, and we honour it.\n- Unsure about anything legal in Victoria? Ask Damian or Luke before, not after.",
  "source": "15-minute-appraisal--workbook-the-15-minute-appraisal.md"
});

window.LAUNCHPAD_CONTENT.templates.push({
  "id": "geo-data-definition",
  "title": "What counts as one piece of geo data",
  "kind": "checklist",
  "when": "Check before you log a contact; it counts toward your 300 only if every line in the first list is true.",
  "body": "Office guidance. Jess's Train To Prospect sheet collects name, address and phone number; the letters need email and settlement date too. This is the office's definition for the 300.\n\n## One piece of geo data is\n\n- A NEW contact record. The person was not already in the Ray White Mildura CRM. Search by address and by surname before you add.\n- The owner (or the decision maker) of a property inside your farm. Not a tenant, not a tradie on site.\n- Their name. At minimum a first name and surname you could use in a letter.\n- A way to reach them: a mobile or landline number, or an email address. Both is better.\n- The property address in the farm, matched to the record.\n- Added to the CRM the same day, with the source (door, phone session, open home, OFI callback, referral) and the date.\n\n## Capture where you can (it makes the letters work)\n\n- Email, if you got the phone. Phone, if you got the email.\n- Settlement date, or roughly when they bought. The 7 Year Letter runs on it.\n- Precinct or street group, for the Appraisal Drive calendar.\n- Lead group: Skeptic (no stated need), Audience (thinking about it, 2 to 12 months), Advocate (0 to 2 months, or asked for an appraisal).\n- Their answer to \"any real estate needs in the next 12 months?\", in their words, in the notes.\n- Anything personal that helps the next call: the kids' footy, the new deck, the dog's name.\n\n## What does not count toward the 300\n\n- A name with no phone and no email. Log it, but it is a lead to go back for, not geo data.\n- An existing contact you updated. Good work, still not new data.\n- A buyer or a renter who does not own in the farm. Add them to the database as a buyer; they do not count toward the 300.\n- A property outside the farm. Add it to the database; it does not count.\n- Anyone who asked not to be contacted. Mark the address \"do not contact\" and move on.\n\n## How you collect it (Jess's Train To Prospect script)\n\n- Introduce. Reason: the lead magnet (\"Your friends and neighbours have loved receiving it\"). Offer: \"If I can grab your details I'd love to send one to you\". Collect: name, address, phone number. Seek opportunity: any real estate needs in the next 12 months. Next steps: \"Thanks for that. I'll have that (lead magnet) to you shortly.\"\n- Then send what you promised, within 48 hours. That is the first touch, and it is what makes the next call easy. Telling them what you will send is also how they agree to hear from you.\n\n## The maths\n\n- 300 over 12 weeks is about 25 a week, 5 a working day. Two green call sessions plus one street of doors gets there.\n- Jess's figure: each geographical contact is worth about $500 a year (1000 contacts x 5% transact x $10K). Your Mildura figure uses the office average commission; work it out with Damian or Luke.",
  "source": "prospecting-scripts--prospecting-scripts.md"
});

